rgozzo, itself from an onomatopoeic root garg- or from Vulgar Latin *gurgutia or *gargutium. Another etymology proposed is *guttium, variant of Latin guttur (“throat”), confused with guttus (“jug”). Alternatively, possibly from a derivative Vulgar Latin *gusia (compare French gos"},{"parola":"troia","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"Of uncertain origin; alternatively; or possibly","significato":"sow (female pig)","termine_origine":"","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","alternative":[{"codice":"la-lat","nome":"Late Latin","termine":"troia","categoria":"ereditato"},{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"","categoria":"ereditato"},{"codice":"cel","nome":"Celtic","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-pro","nome":"Proto-Celtic","termine":"*trāglo-","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"testo":"From Late Latin troia, troja, of uncertain origin, but possibly: * from Latin (porcum de) Troia (“(hog of) Troy”), after the Trojan horse, * or alternatively of Celtic origin, from a Gaulish root *trogja, from a root meaning “to pull” and thence also “fertile,\" Proto-Celtic *trāglo-, for which see L"},{"parola":"valigia","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"Unclear; Possibly; either","significato":"suitcase","termine_origine":"*valisia","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*valisia","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","alternative":[{"codice":"la-med","nome":"Medieval Latin","termine":"valesia","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"la-lat","nome":"Late Latin","termine":"valisia","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*valisia","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-pro","nome":"Proto-Celtic","termine":"*wal-","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"ine-pro","nome":"Proto-Indo-European","termine":"*welH-","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"ar","nome":"Arabic","termine":"وَلِيهَة","traslitterazione":"walīha","categoria":"prestito"}],"testo":"Borrowed from a language of Northern Italy (cf. Venetan valís, Lombard valiza), from Medieval Latin valesia, valixia, from Late Latin valisia, possibly from Gaulish *valisia (“leather bag”), from Proto-Celtic *wal- (“to enclose, surround”), from Proto-Indo-European *welH-. Or, possibly from Arabic و"}]}